 ## Background
-Surface Nets was developed as an extension to Marching Cubes to place intersecting vertices in the middle of volume voxels rather than at the edges, leading to a smoother output surface [S1]. Marching Tetrahedra was separately developed as an extension to Marching Cubes to solve an ambiguity in that algorithm [S1]. Dual Contouring was later published in the 2002 SIGGRAPH proceedings by Ju and Losasso as an extension to both Surface Nets and Marching Cubes, retaining a dual vertex within the voxel but interpolating its position using edge crossings and normals to retain sharp features [S1]. The self-intersection reduction technique is applied during the export of Surface Nets geometry, particularly for OBJ file generation [S2, unofficial].
+The self-intersection reduction technique was introduced alongside extensions to Surface Nets for multi-label segmentations to preserve sharp boundaries [S2, unofficial]. It is applied during the OBJ export phase of Surface Nets geometry generation [S2, unofficial]. Surface Nets itself was developed as an extension to Marching Cubes to place intersecting vertices in the middle of volume voxels rather than at the edges, leading to a smoother output surface [S1]. Marching Tetrahedra was separately developed as an extension to Marching Cubes to resolve topological ambiguities [S1]. Dual Contouring was later published in the 2002 SIGGRAPH proceedings by Ju and Losasso as an extension to both Surface Nets and Marching Cubes, retaining a dual vertex within the voxel but interpolating its position using edge crossings and normals to retain sharp features [S1].
 
